What is this project and why does it matter?
Imagine you're building a multiplayer game like chess, a real-time trading card game, or a strategy game where 4 players move units on a map simultaneously. The moment two players do something at the exact same instant, you have a problem. Who moved first? Was that move legal? What if someone loses internet for 3 seconds — do they get kicked out? What if someone hacks the client to move twice as fast?These are not game logic problems. They are distributed systems problems. This project is the backend engine that solves all of them — the invisible infrastructure every multiplayer game needs but rarely teaches.

Why we're building this?
Problem 1: Concurrency. Two players send actions at the same millisecond. Your server gets both. Which one happened first? TCP gives you packets in arrival order, but network latency means arrival order ≠ action order. You need a way to assign a canonical global sequence to every action.
Problem 2: State divergence. Player A on a 200ms ping sees the board differently than Player B on a 10ms ping. If both their clients are authoritative about what's "true," they'll disagree. The fix: only the server decides truth. Clients are just rendering terminals. This is the authoritative server pattern
Cheating. If the client sends "I moved my unit 10 squares," can you trust that? No. The server must re-validate every action against the real game state. The client can lie; the server's state cannot be corrupted from outside.
Problem 4: Reconnection. A player drops for 8 seconds. When they come back, what do they see? You need a snapshot + event log so you can replay exactly what happened in those 8 seconds and fast-forward their state to current.
Problem 5: Optimistic updates. Waiting for server confirmation before showing your own move feels laggy. Good clients apply the move locally immediately ("optimistic"), then correct if the server says no. This reconciliation is subtle — what if 3 server ticks happened while your optimistic move was in-flight?
